-- title:
--   Demands generated by an action profile
-- statement:
--   In the supply-chain model of Iyengar, Saleh, Sethuraman and Wang, two vendors $h,l$ supply $4m$ manufacturers, each of a type $(q_i,\xi_i)\in\{A,B\}\times\{h,l\}$, and a unit mass of consumers buys from the manufacturers or takes an outside option.
--
--   For an action profile, the demands $s_i$ are the consumer demands (7) at the profile's public history, under the true type assignment.
-- source:
--   Iyengar, Saleh, Sethuraman, Wang, Economics of Permissioned Blockchain Adoption, SSRN 3602376 (version of 2022-08-19), §3.2, p. 13 (PDF p. 14); display (7), p. 12

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PermBlockchain_Transfers_demand
import Definitions.Def_PermBlockchain_Transfers_histOf

namespace PermBlockchain.Transfers

/-- **The demands `s_i` generated by an action profile.**
Iyengar, Saleh, Sethuraman, Wang, *Economics of Permissioned Blockchain Adoption*,
SSRN 3602376 (version of 2022-08-19), §3.2, p. 13 (PDF p. 14): "s_i ≥ 0 denotes the endogenously determined consumer demand for
manufacturer i. Note that s_i depends upon (a_i, a_{−i})"; (7), p. 12.

`sOf ν fee w θ α` is `demand ν fee w θ (histOf α)`: consumers respond to the public history of
`α` under the true assignment `θ`. -/
noncomputable def sOf {m : ℕ} (ν : MeasureTheory.Measure ℝ) (fee : FeeRule m) (w : PurchaseRule m)
    (θ : Mfr m → MType) (α : Mfr m → Act) : Mfr m → ℝ :=
  fun i => demand ν fee w θ (histOf α) i

end PermBlockchain.Transfers


